From 2cdbacce2391795da65560d83df534ef024cdcc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GitHub Copilot <copilot@github.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Nov 2025 17:41:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Improve MAUI quick start documentation

- Update .NET version from 7.0 to 9.0 (7.0 is out of support)
- Add Prerequisites section with MAUI workload installation
- Add command-line alternatives for Visual Studio commands
- Provide both Visual Studio and CLI project creation options
- Add command-line package installation option
- Enhance Step 5 description with expected behavior
- Add note about platform-specific build requirements

Based on validation testing in Mapsui/quickstart repository.
---
 docs/general/markdown/index.md | 30 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/general/markdown/index.md b/docs/general/markdown/index.md
index 3717b6479..ff189b380 100644
--- a/docs/general/markdown/index.md
+++ b/docs/general/markdown/index.md
@@ -21,14 +21,36 @@ Try the quick-start for your favorite framework below.
 
     === "MAUI"
 
-        **Step 1:** Create a new .NET 7.0 MAUI application in Visual Studio.
+        **Prerequisites:**
+        
+        - .NET 9.0 SDK or later installed
+        - For Visual Studio users: MAUI workload selected during installation
+        - For command-line users: Install MAUI workload with `dotnet workload install maui`
 
-        **Step 2:** In the package manager console type:
+        **Step 1:** Create a new .NET 9.0 MAUI application.
+
+        In Visual Studio: File > New > Project > .NET MAUI App
+
+        Or using the command line:
+        
+        ```console
+        dotnet new maui -n MyMapsuiApp
+        ```
+
+        **Step 2:** Add the Mapsui.Maui package.
+
+        In Visual Studio Package Manager Console:
 
         ```console
         PM> Install-Package Mapsui.Maui
         ```
 
+        Or using the command line:
+
+        ```console
+        dotnet add package Mapsui.Maui
+        ```
+
         **Step 3:** IMPORTANT: In MauiProgram.cs add **.UseSkiaSharp()** to the builder like this:
 
         ```csharp
@@ -61,7 +83,9 @@ Try the quick-start for your favorite framework below.
         ```
 
         **Step 5:**
-        Run it and you should see a map of the world.
+        Run it and you should see a map of the world displaying OpenStreetMap tiles. You can pan and zoom the map.
+
+        **Note:** The default MAUI template targets multiple platforms (Android, iOS, Windows, macOS). Building for iOS/macOS requires a Mac with Xcode installed. Building for Windows requires Windows with Visual Studio.
     
 	=== "Avalonia"
         
